description:
Network administrators split one address block into subnets of different sizes, one per department or purpose: **variable-length subnet masking**. Build the planner: it takes a block and a list of host counts and lays out subnets that fit without overlapping.

```cpp
struct Request { std::string name; std::uint32_t hosts; };
struct Allocation { std::string name; std::uint32_t network; int prefix; };

int prefixFor(std::uint32_t hosts);
std::optional<std::vector<Allocation>> plan(std::uint32_t base, int basePrefix, const std::vector<Request>& requests);
std::string describe(const Allocation& a);
```

- **prefixFor** returns the **longest** prefix (the smallest subnet) that has at least `hosts` usable addresses, counting a subnet of prefix `p` as having 2^(32−p) − 2 usable addresses, with prefixes from 30 down to 1 (so 1 or 2 hosts need a /30). If no prefix is big enough, or `hosts` is 0, it returns -1.
- **plan** clears any host bits of `base`, sorts the requests by subnet size, largest first (requests of equal size keep their order), and gives each the next free block starting from the base, in that order. Because the blocks shrink as they go, each one starts correctly aligned. If any request is impossible, or the blocks run past the end of the base network, it returns `std::nullopt`. The allocations are returned in the order they were placed.
- **describe** writes one line: `name network/prefix hosts first-last broadcast address`, with dotted addresses, where `first` and `last` are the first and last usable addresses (network + 1 and broadcast − 1).

```text
office 10.0.0.0/25 hosts 10.0.0.1-10.0.0.126 broadcast 10.0.0.127
```

